# Philosophy Lab

**AI-written philosophy, with the drafts, criticism, decisions, and conversations kept alongside it.**

Codex built this repository and wrote its guide. The essays are AI-written too: Codex, Claude, and mixed-model contributions are credited in their histories. Human prompts and source quotations are preserved as quotations.

The project began on **September 7, 2026**, with the AI Philosophy Competition. It grew into a study of how to find worthwhile questions, develop arguments, use criticism, and keep an honest record of what worked and what failed.

**100 original essays · 7 later essays · 10 independently checked · 3 recommended, plus 2 reserves**

[Read the five short cards](docs/final-shortlist.md) · [Read the ten full texts](reading/20261018/README.md) · [Browse every essay](essays/README.md) · [Follow the history](docs/timeline.md) · [Open the logs](logs/README.md)

## Where the work stands

The **22 → 10 selection is complete**. Its accepted record contains 110 individual reviews and 50 reviews of disputed pairs. Three neutral and two judge-profile reviewers per text preserved the agreed 60/40 balance. The [selection guide](docs/selection.md) explains each decision and the remaining objections.

**October 31:** all twenty independent Codex Astra / ultra audits are accepted: an argument check and a separate literature/source check for each exact manuscript. The coordinator recommends **E050, E009 and E012**, with **E078 and E041** in reserve. [Five short cards and the remaining limits](docs/final-shortlist.md) explain the choice. No manuscript was rewritten or submitted during verification.

The conversation export has also been reconciled against the available local record. The [log guide](logs/README.md) explains what was recovered, the exact cutoffs, and what remains unavailable or excluded.

## How we got here

Dates below describe the work itself, in California time. The GitHub repository was first assembled on October 13.

| When | Milestone |
| --- | --- |
| **Sep 7** | The project begins; a dedicated laboratory and conversation record are established. |
| **Sep 8–10** | Three independent batches produce the first **10, then 30, then 50 essays**; the third tests deliberately plain language. |
| **Sep 11–20** | Judge-guided and Claude work begin. Repeated bridge failures lead to a smaller CLI runner and a successful bounded live test. |
| **Sep 21–24** | Essay biographies are connected to the record. The creative, Claude, imaginative, and judge-reviewed branches close with their failures and limits preserved. |
| **Sep 25–27** | Six different methods take the collection to **100 essays**. The hundredth completion is recorded late on September 27. |
| **Oct 7–11** | A full-panel midterm, revision trial, evaluator diagnostics, and literature checks narrow the next research questions. |
| **Oct 12–15** | Five follow-up programs include 300 idea attempts, targeted argument work, a rejection check, and **seven new essays**. |
| **Oct 16–17** | The follow-up work is consolidated; **22 exact candidate versions** are prepared. |
| **Oct 18–19** | The 60/40 review process selects **ten essays**, keeping eight main places and two wildcards. |
| **Oct 31** | Log coverage and the full research history are audited; twenty independent checks support a recommendation of three essays and two reserves. |

